  "SourceElements": [
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L07-ACTIVITY-001",
      "SourceSection": "7.1: Halving and Doubling",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A colony of microbes doubles in population every 6 hours. Explain why we could say that the population grows by a factor of \\(\\sqrt[6]{2}\\) every hour.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L07-ACTIVITY-002",
      "SourceSection": "7.1: Halving and Doubling",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A bacteria population decreases by a factor of \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) every 4 hours. Explain why we could also say that the population decays by a factor of \\(\\sqrt[4]{\\frac12}\\) every hour.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L07-ACTIVITY-003",
      "SourceSection": "7.2: Radiocarbon Dating",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Carbon-14 is used to find the age of certain artifacts and fossils. It has a half-life of 5,730 years, so if an object has carbon-14, it loses half of it every 5,730 years.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L07-ACTIVITY-004",
      "SourceSection": "7.2: Radiocarbon Dating",
      "SourceExcerpt": "At a certain point in time, a fossil had 3 picograms (a trillionth of a gram) of carbon-14. Complete the table with the missing mass of carbon-14 and years.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L07-ACTIVITY-005",
      "SourceSection": "7.2: Radiocarbon Dating",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What do the 2.5, \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\), and 5,730 mean in this situation?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L07-ACTIVITY-006",
      "SourceSection": "7.2: Radiocarbon Dating",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Would more or less than 0.1 picogram of carbon-14 remain in this fossil today? Explain how you know.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L07-ACTIVITY-007",
      "SourceSection": "7.2: Radiocarbon Dating",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A scientist uses the expression \\((2.5) \\boldcdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{\\frac{t}{5,730}}\\) to model the number of picograms of carbon-14 remaining in a different fossil \\(t\\) years after 20,000 BC.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L07-ACTIVITY-008",
      "SourceSection": "7.3: Old Manuscripts",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The half-life of carbon-14 is about 5,730 years.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L07-ACTIVITY-009",
      "SourceSection": "7.3: Old Manuscripts",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Pythagoras lived between 600 BCE and 500 BCE. Explain why the age of a papyrus from the time of Pythagoras is about half of a carbon-14 half-life.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L07-ACTIVITY-010",
      "SourceSection": "7.3: Old Manuscripts",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Someone claims they have a papyrus scroll written by Pythagoras. Testing shows the scroll has 85% of its original amount of carbon-14 remaining. Explain why the scroll is likely a fake.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L07-ACTIVITY-011",
      "SourceSection": "7.3: Old Manuscripts",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A copy of the Gutenberg Bible was made around 1450. Would more or less than 90% of the carbon-14 remain in the paper today? Explain how you know.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L07-ACTIVITY-012",
      "SourceSection": "7.3: Old Manuscripts",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Some substances change over time through a process called radioactive decay, and their rate of decay can be measured or estimated. Let’s take sodium-22 as an example.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L07-ACTIVITY-013",
      "SourceSection": "7.3: Old Manuscripts",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Suppose a scientist finds 4 nanograms of sodium-22 in a sample of an artifact. (One nanogram is 1 billionth, or \\(10^{\\text-9}\\), of a gram.) Approximately every 3 years, half of the sodium-22 decays. We can represent this change with a…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L07-ACTIVITY-014",
      "SourceSection": "7.3: Old Manuscripts",
      "SourceExcerpt": "This can also be represented by an equation. If the function \\(f\\) gives the number of nanograms of sodium remaining after \\(t\\) years then \\(\\displaystyle f(t) = 4 \\boldcdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{\\frac{t}{3}}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L07-ACTIVITY-015",
      "SourceSection": "7.3: Old Manuscripts",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The 4 represents the number of nanograms in the sample when it was first measured, while the \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) and 3 show that the amount of sodium is cut in half every 3 years, because if you increase \\(t\\) by 3, you increase the exponent…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L07-ACTIVITY-016",
      "SourceSection": "7.3: Old Manuscripts",
      "SourceExcerpt": "How much of the sodium remains after one year? Using the equation, we find \\(f(1) = 4 \\boldcdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\). This is about 3.2 nanograms.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L07-ACTIVITY-017",
      "SourceSection": "7.3: Old Manuscripts",
      "SourceExcerpt": "About how many years after the first measurement will there be about 0.015 nanogram of sodium-22? One way to find out is by extending the table and multiplying the mass of sodium-22 by \\(\\frac12\\) each time. If we multiply 0.5 nanogram…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L07-ACTIVITY-018",
      "SourceSection": "7.3: Old Manuscripts",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Archaeologists and scientists use exponential functions to help estimate the ages of ancient things.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
